okay, so you mentioned writing there from maybe the business marketing and the discovery side of things.

35
00:02:34,630 --> 00:02:39,430
are there any other aspects of writing that you feel are important for software developers to know about or learn about?

36
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:43,190
absolutely, you know we're not just writing copy for a website.

37
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:48,869
or even writing a technical article explaining how to use a piece of open source code.

38
00:02:48,879 --> 00:02:54,869
you also write documentation memos to your team emails every day.

39
00:02:54,869 --> 00:02:58,790
if you're communicating in any sort of asynchronous fashion it's probably through writing.

40
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:04,550
and so in order to effectively work with other people work in a team.

41
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:09,110
show knowledge across teams or within a team about a project on board people.

42
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:13,830
there are so many different things that really strong written content can help you do.

43
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:17,030
and when i say written content i'm speaking much more broadly.

44
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:20,869
than just the sort of thing you might see on a company's blog.

45
00:03:20,869 --> 00:03:25,110
you know this this concept encapsulates things like weed me's documentations.

46
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:31,750
and basically every other written artifact even you know the history in your slack channels.

47
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:33,910
yeah, then that's interesting.

48
00:03:33,910 --> 00:03:37,670
so you did make a big assumption that people are actually writing documentation which i might question.

49
00:03:37,670 --> 00:03:41,750
but you went down to the level of things you say in slack.

50
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:43,270
and i was about to ask you so kind of segues perfectly.

51
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:49,750
what about somebody who says to you my code is self-documenting and it speaks for itself?

52
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:53,030
well, if that's true that's awesome.

53
00:03:53,030 --> 00:04:00,630
but i think that even if that's the case you're still going to be doing things like commit messages.

54
00:04:00,630 --> 00:04:02,550
or even little comments even if it's just to yourself.

55
00:04:02,560 --> 00:04:06,070
and you can think about it like tweeting almost.

56
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:10,949
like if you've ever written a tweet and you have to encapsulate an idea in this very small amount of space.

57
00:04:10,949 --> 00:04:14,309
code is an incredibly information dense way to light.

58
00:04:14,319 --> 00:04:17,670
and technical content relies on code samples.

59
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:24,310
that sometimes you know you don't have to explain every single line of def funk vars.

60
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:27,430
is self-documenting to anyone who knows the language they understand this is a function.

61
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:32,390
unless this is a very simple introduction to the language you don't need to explain that.

62
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:34,469
you're just wasting people's time if you do so.

63
00:04:34,479 --> 00:04:39,909
so just because code is this very information-dense way of communicating.

64
00:04:39,919 --> 00:04:43,909
it means exactly what you want it to mean.

65
00:04:43,909 --> 00:04:47,990
that doesn't mean that even very good clean code doesn't need further explanation.

66
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:51,110
because the code is going to do a great job of telling people what it does.

67
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:55,189
but it's not necessarily going to do a great job of telling people why it exists.

68
00:04:55,199 --> 00:04:59,030
who it's useful for what the purpose is behind writing it.

69
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:03,110
and these are all of the things that even very small pieces of documentation.

70
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:07,189
like comments and commit logs these sort of tweet link things.

71
00:05:07,189 --> 00:05:10,790
that are a really fun challenge to write and make concise and meaningful.

72
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:14,390
all the way up to entire manuals and books about how to do things.

73
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:20,469
everything along that scale tends to be less concerned with breaking down exactly what happens.

74
00:05:20,469 --> 00:05:25,510
and tends to be more concerned with talking about why and how these artifacts exist.

75
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:31,749
so in your experience do you sense that there's places where the documentation is insufficient?

76
00:05:31,749 --> 00:05:36,230
that you might recommend that it be increased?

77
00:05:36,230 --> 00:05:40,790
absolutely, and i mean i'm certainly guilty of this in my own projects.

78
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:43,110
both in my personal life and at work you know i'm no saint.

79
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:48,310
when it comes to sitting down and really writing out what happened and why.

80
00:05:48,310 --> 00:05:52,629
and i think that in some cases this practice is more important.

81
00:05:52,629 --> 00:05:57,909
if you think about something like a retrospective or a postmortem on an outage or some security flaw.

82
00:05:57,919 --> 00:06:02,309
these documents can be really important for fostering a sense of understanding.

83
00:06:02,319 --> 00:06:06,150
and making sure that mistakes aren't repeated.

84
00:06:06,150 --> 00:06:12,309
all of which to say just because the documentation might be lacking universally.

85
00:06:12,309 --> 00:06:16,710
you know we can always have more documentation that doesn't really help so much.

86
00:06:16,710 --> 00:06:20,469
because we only have a limited amount of time to work on these projects.

87
00:06:20,469 --> 00:06:21,990
everyone has deadlines to ship by.

88
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:28,870
and so it's about thinking about precisely who the audience for certain pieces of technical communication might be.

89
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:36,629
and what we can do that's going to give us the highest return on the time that we invest in creating it.

90
00:06:36,629 --> 00:06:39,029
in order to communicate important information about the work that we do.

91
00:06:39,039 --> 00:06:42,309
it's interesting you mentioned the audience.

92
00:06:42,309 --> 00:06:45,670
speak a little bit about an audience focus versus a center focus.

93
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:50,230
does that play into your thoughts on how software engineers should write about their work?

94
00:06:50,240 --> 00:06:54,550
having your audience in mind is the number one way to improve your writing.

95
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:57,189
and here's why.

96
00:06:57,189 --> 00:07:01,830
let's say you're programming something let's say you know you're writing an api.

97
00:07:01,830 --> 00:07:03,990
and it's going to return some information.

98
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,830
it doesn't matter how well you implement it. it doesn't matter how scalable your deployment is.

99
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:11,589
all of this other stuff doesn't matter.

100
00:07:11,599 --> 00:07:17,270
if the information that you are conveying is not going to the right place.

101
00:07:17,280 --> 00:07:21,110
so if you think about it it's kind of the same for writing.

102
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:25,270
you are creating something for an audience.

103
00:07:25,270 --> 00:07:27,189
that audience might be one person you might be emailing your boss.

104
00:07:27,199 --> 00:07:28,950
great your boss is your audience.

105
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:32,070
that audience might be a huge number of people.

106
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,629
you know you work on django or wales or some huge open source framework.

107
00:07:36,639 --> 00:07:44,070
and you're editing the introductory tutorial that tens of thousands of people read every year to get into this project.

108
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:48,150
it doesn't matter you still have an audience every time you write.

109
00:07:48,150 --> 00:07:50,550
an ad audience i should even mention could be yourself.

110
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,309
if you're writing yourself a note or a memo about how something works.

111
00:07:54,309 --> 00:07:57,029
or why you made a particular choice while implementing something.

112
00:07:57,039 --> 00:08:01,749
and so if you don't have a good understanding of who your audience is.

113
00:08:01,759 --> 00:08:10,230
no amount of craft in terms of you know having great sentences or having really clear explanations is going to fix it.

114
00:08:10,230 --> 00:08:12,309
if you're not giving them the information that they need.

115
00:08:12,319 --> 00:08:19,110
and so when i'm writing to a technical audience i'm able to say things that i couldn't say to a non-technical audience.

116
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:24,869
when i'm writing to try to convince a co-worker that my implementation is correct.

117
00:08:24,869 --> 00:08:28,469
while they're performing a code review that's going to be very different.

118
00:08:28,469 --> 00:08:32,310
than trying to strike a more pedagogical tone.

119
00:08:32,310 --> 00:08:37,269
as i'm trying to explain a piece of code in a technical tutorial for wide audience.

120
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:44,149
and so every single aspect of your writing changes depending on who you're writing for.

121
00:08:44,149 --> 00:08:47,190
and that's why understanding your audience is an incredibly critical step.

122
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:50,870
and i love how you pointed out that you might be the audience.

123
00:08:50,880 --> 00:08:52,870
it's always terrifying to do a git blame.

124
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:57,030
and then discover that you're the person responsible for the state of that bit of code.

125
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:00,389
and it reminds me of a funny scene in the simpsons.

126
00:09:00,389 --> 00:09:05,509
where homer has a bottle of vodka and a tub of mayonnaise. and he's about to consume both of them.

127
00:09:05,519 --> 00:09:09,750
and marge says something along the lines of homer i don't think that's such a great idea.

128
00:09:09,750 --> 00:09:13,030
and homer says oh that's a problem for future homer. boy i don't envy that guy.

129
00:09:13,040 --> 00:09:16,790
and in a similar fashion i think a lot of us you know we hate our future selves.

130
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:18,470
if we don't properly document things.

131
00:09:18,470 --> 00:09:21,430
i think is maybe a takeaway of what you're saying there?

132
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:28,070
definitely, and you know a lot of people ask me how to improve at writing.

133
00:09:28,070 --> 00:09:31,829
and one of the best ways to do that is you know just practice more.

134
00:09:31,829 --> 00:09:33,590
and we can get more into that later.

135
00:09:33,600 --> 00:09:36,870
but one of the best ways to practice is just write to yourself.

136
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:44,070
you know keep a developer log keep a running documentation on your personal projects.

137
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:51,350
i don't always do these things but when i do them that's when i see the biggest return.

138
00:09:51,350 --> 00:09:54,150
in terms of learning more from the things that i'm working on.

139
00:09:54,150 --> 00:09:55,990
and then i'm able to pick them up later.

140
00:09:55,990 --> 00:10:00,310
and really understand where i was and keep going where i left off.

141
00:10:00,310 --> 00:10:03,829
now it seems like you know what you mentioned is true in so many pursuits in life.

142
00:10:03,829 --> 00:10:06,550
if you want to play guitar you play guitar.

143
00:10:06,550 --> 00:10:08,710
if you want to surf you surf.

144
00:10:08,710 --> 00:10:11,990
but i think sometimes people might feel like it's so daunting that it's difficult to get started.

145
00:10:11,990 --> 00:10:15,269
and especially with writing i think people might struggle.

146
00:10:15,269 --> 00:10:18,150
and i'm curious if your experience if you've seen this as well.

147
00:10:18,150 --> 00:10:19,990
where people maybe struggle with where to get started.

148
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,590
and it sounds like you're saying is just just get started and start doing it.

149
00:10:23,590 --> 00:10:27,269
but how do you help people to overcome maybe that resistance.

150
00:10:27,269 --> 00:10:31,910
or that concern about whether what they write is going to be received well.

151
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:34,230
or is going to accomplish the task for which they're writing it?

152
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:40,389
yeah, so let me turn that question around on you actually with a software perspective.

153
00:10:40,389 --> 00:10:45,190
when you are creating a piece of software you know a new web app or something.

154
00:10:45,190 --> 00:10:48,069
you've opened up your ide you've got a totally blank page.

155
00:10:48,079 --> 00:10:49,590
what's the first thing you do?

156
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:55,990
i usually do a readme file for me to kind of give me some framing and tasks of what i need to do first.

157
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:59,990
exactly, and it's the exact same thing that you'd want to do when you're writing.

158
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:03,750
because the enemy is the blank page you just have to get something on there.

159
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:10,550
so i approach writing code and writing articles or even an entire book in exactly the same way.

160
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:13,829
it's just a project that needs to be broken down into steps.

161
00:11:13,839 --> 00:11:16,069
after the readme what i do generally.

162
00:11:16,069 --> 00:11:21,350
i usually work with a framework so you know i'll do django admin start project for example.

163
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:24,790
and that's going to create some boilerplate is going to create some structure.

164
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:30,230
and as soon as you have structure that's going to help out a ton in creating your content.

165
00:11:30,230 --> 00:11:33,590
boy that'd be great if we had a cli for kicking off our papers in a similar fashion.

166
00:11:33,590 --> 00:11:36,630
but it would be excellent.

167
00:11:36,630 --> 00:11:42,310
so you can create for yourself a similar structure in writing by creating an outline.

168
00:11:42,310 --> 00:11:44,069
that talks about first of course your audience.

169
00:11:44,079 --> 00:11:48,630
and then the information that you want to cover how these things relate to each other.

170
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:52,710
figure out a meaningful order to put these ideas on the page.

171
00:11:52,720 --> 00:11:59,110
and so even you know five bullet points is so much better than a blank page for getting started.

172
00:11:59,110 --> 00:12:03,829
and then you just come back to it you just sit down every day you know don't try and force it all out.

173
00:12:03,839 --> 00:12:09,670
if it's if it's your first time trying to write a 2000 word technical tutorial. don't try and do that in one sitting.

174
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:14,310
do it for half an hour every day for a week and see what you can come up with.

175
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:18,470
and so it's exactly the same skills that we've all built up.

176
00:12:18,470 --> 00:12:23,990
working on software engineering projects of taking a big complicated thing and breaking it down into achievable tasks.

177
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,231
you just have to do the same thing with your writing. that makes sense.

178
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:31,910
so we've kind of moved into this a little bit i think.

179
00:12:31,910 --> 00:12:34,870
but in general what do you feel are the elements of effective technical writing?

180
00:12:34,870 --> 00:12:40,389
and technical writers you've mentioned a couple but are there others that you would want to include?

181
00:12:40,389 --> 00:12:48,310
yeah, we can basically break it down on the axes of the two elements of this phrase technical writing.

182
00:12:48,310 --> 00:12:50,389
those technical and those writing.

183
00:12:50,389 --> 00:12:52,470
so to start with the technical that's pretty simple.

184
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:55,829
you have to know a little bit about what you're writing about.

185
00:12:55,839 --> 00:13:01,030
and that doesn't mean you have to be the industry expert in a thing to write about that thing.

186
00:13:01,030 --> 00:13:05,990
i've written about a ton of stuff where you know i maybe have a yellow belt in these concepts.

187
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,910
i'm beyond the beginner level i have something to teach.

188
00:13:09,920 --> 00:13:14,230
but i'm not an expert i'm not the person who wrote the open source repository.

189
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:17,269
i'm just someone who's been using it for a while.

190
00:13:17,279 --> 00:13:21,030
something that in my interview with angel gorisma for the book.

191
00:13:21,030 --> 00:13:24,790
something that he talked about was having really authentic content.

192
00:13:24,790 --> 00:13:28,710
and the way he and his team achieved that was just a ton of research.

193
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:38,790
even if you don't have the capacity of an entire team of technical content people to do research with you. can still put in the time to do example projects.

194
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:42,470
play with whatever technology you're going to be writing about.

195
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:49,269
and sort of figure out the technical side to make sure that you have a strong understanding to convey to your readers.

196
00:13:49,279 --> 00:13:54,389
i'm going to assume though that the listeners of this podcast more or less have the technical side figured out.

197
00:13:54,399 --> 00:13:58,230
it's the writing side that people can get tripped up on.

198
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:04,870
and so with that good writing has a lot of definitions and is partially a matter of taste.

199
00:14:04,870 --> 00:14:12,230
you know my english professors at grinnell would certainly hate the type of writing that i do for clients.

200
00:14:12,230 --> 00:14:15,430
and my editors you know i don't know if they love it but they think it's good enough to publish.

201
00:14:15,440 --> 00:14:19,269
so there's always that matter of taste.

202
00:14:19,279 --> 00:14:25,030
and when i say taste you might be thinking who has taste the audience?

203
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:29,670
exactly, it all comes back to that who you're writing for determines how you write.

204
00:14:29,670 --> 00:14:32,150
in terms of not universal but as close as i can get.

205
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:35,910
um having clio approachable syntax.

206
00:14:35,910 --> 00:14:41,030
one of the great things about computer science is that there are a lot of people in this field.

207
00:14:41,030 --> 00:14:43,350
who speak english as a second third fourth whatever language.

208
00:14:43,360 --> 00:14:48,710
and so having content that's approachable to everyone regardless of their background.

209
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:53,990
in you know literary english for example that's really important.

210
00:14:53,990 --> 00:14:59,269
so you know that doesn't mean you can't use precise terms to mean exactly what you want to say.

211
00:14:59,269 --> 00:15:03,750
but you know don't throw in 10 words for the sake of having them.

212
00:15:03,750 --> 00:15:07,430
or showing off your big vocabulary that's something that i definitely try to avoid.

213
00:15:07,430 --> 00:15:12,310
and having nice clear syntax having someone edit your work.

214
00:15:12,320 --> 00:15:15,670
even if they're not technical just to make sure that it sounds good.

215
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:19,189
reading your work aloud to make sure that it sounds conversational.

216
00:15:19,189 --> 00:15:24,230
sounds like the sort of thing that is reasonable for a person to have written.

217
00:15:24,230 --> 00:15:27,350
all of these things are steps that you can take to make your writing better.

218
00:15:27,360 --> 00:15:31,350
but ultimately like i said good writing is a matter of taste.

