00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:36,870
and the way that you develop both your taste and your ability to match that taste is just practice.

220
00:15:36,880 --> 00:15:42,710
now the recommendations you made there sound like they primarily apply to larger works.

221
00:15:42,710 --> 00:15:46,150
so how do some of the elements of what you're describing fit in more of the smaller scenarios?

222
00:15:46,160 --> 00:15:49,990
comments and code bullets in a readme or commit messages.

223
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:54,470
how does good writing demonstrate its importance in those contexts?

224
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:58,629
writing really really short things has been a challenge for a long time.

225
00:15:58,639 --> 00:16:04,069
for this we have to turn to some of the skills that you know a poet might use when they're writing a haiku.

226
00:16:04,079 --> 00:16:07,430
it's not just about what you say it's about what you don't say.

227
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:09,829
so when you're writing a commit message.

228
00:16:09,839 --> 00:16:16,470
good writing is leaving out extraneous detail leaving out words that you don't need.

229
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:23,030
and just focusing on conveying exactly the information you want to say.

230
00:16:23,030 --> 00:16:25,829
to exactly who needs to heal it in the minimum amount of time possible.

231
00:16:25,839 --> 00:16:32,949
even if it's just a sentence you can still write it down read it aloud change it if it sounds bad.

232
00:16:32,959 --> 00:16:36,629
that said if you're writing just a commit message.

233
00:16:36,629 --> 00:16:41,749
it's important to understand that i'm not saying you need to spend 30 minutes crafting a perfect poetic commit message.

234
00:16:41,759 --> 00:16:48,470
that says i added five lines it will fix the checkout bug it's snowing on mount fiji.

235
00:16:48,470 --> 00:16:51,749
you know you don't need to write a haiku in your which i know that wasn't a haiku.

236
00:16:51,749 --> 00:16:55,590
but you don't need to write a haiku in your github logs every single time.

237
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:02,470
it's just more about getting the job done with a decent amount of effort and moving on.

238
00:17:02,470 --> 00:17:04,309
doing it again doing it again until it feels natural.

239
00:17:04,309 --> 00:17:05,909
and of course context there matters as well.

240
00:17:05,909 --> 00:17:09,110
if it's your branch and you're just doing work in progress then that's one thing.

241
00:17:09,110 --> 00:17:11,429
but when you're committing it for consumption by others.

242
00:17:11,439 --> 00:17:16,949
hopefully now that audience focus as you said was the number one consideration.

243
00:17:16,949 --> 00:17:19,429
then at that point you're going to have to think differently about that.

244
00:17:19,439 --> 00:17:23,590
any other elements of effective writing that you feel are important?

245
00:17:23,590 --> 00:17:26,789
maybe maybe ones that software developers aren't typically known for.

246
00:17:26,799 --> 00:17:30,150
possibly or maybe have overlooked that would be worth mentioning?

247
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:35,990
there's somewhat of a shared understanding in software development.

248
00:17:35,990 --> 00:17:39,350
that as software developers were somehow exempt from the rules of the load.

249
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:47,270
in terms of just generic decent writing you know proper usage proper punctuation that sort of stuff.

250
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:53,909
and i would encourage everyone to just understand that.

251
00:17:53,909 --> 00:17:59,190
just like we have conventions and explicit rules in various programming languages.

252
00:17:59,190 --> 00:18:01,750
human languages have the same things for the same reasons.

253
00:18:01,760 --> 00:18:05,029
it's just so that we can all understand each other better.

254
00:18:05,029 --> 00:18:10,470
so i think that having you know a decent grasp on whatever language you're writing in.

255
00:18:10,470 --> 00:18:12,630
that gets you 90 of the way there.

256
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:15,750
and the rest of it is just about understanding the audience.

257
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:20,470
and making sure that you have a clear direct message to tell them.

258
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:24,630
so i imagine a lot of what you've shared so far you discuss in your book.

259
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:28,150
which is of course called writing for software developers great name.

260
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:30,630
so why this book and why now?

261
00:18:30,630 --> 00:18:35,430
thank you, uh writing for software developers is a really tough title to live up to.

262
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:40,150
because there's just a lot of information there.

263
00:18:40,150 --> 00:18:42,310
why this book because it didn't exist before.

264
00:18:42,310 --> 00:18:44,310
and when i started out i really needed it.

265
00:18:44,320 --> 00:18:48,310
and why now because i finally have the background and experience.

266
00:18:48,310 --> 00:18:50,549
that made me feel like i was able to write it.

267
00:18:50,559 --> 00:18:57,990
i think that as software development increases in being a remote first profession.

268
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:02,230
where a lot of people are working asynchronously.

269
00:19:02,230 --> 00:19:05,830
even so you know there's there's two types of remote work.

270
00:19:05,830 --> 00:19:08,150
one where you're just on video calls all the time.

271
00:19:08,150 --> 00:19:13,190
and still trying to have the same feeling of everyone working on the same thing at the same time.

272
00:19:13,200 --> 00:19:18,870
another is when i might be in clive iowa working on programming something.

273
00:19:18,870 --> 00:19:25,830
and then i might send it off and someone in i don't know let's say japan is going to pick it up when they wake up.

274
00:19:25,830 --> 00:19:27,350
and it's and it's their business hours.

275
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:33,430
if you're doing asynchronous stuff like that which is of course increasing a lot these days.

276
00:19:33,430 --> 00:19:35,669
then that's where effective writing can help.

277
00:19:35,669 --> 00:19:38,230
so i was thinking about that as i was writing the book.

278
00:19:38,230 --> 00:19:44,549
and the potential for a increase in good content internally.

279
00:19:44,549 --> 00:19:49,110
but the book mostly focuses on good content for things like corporate blogs.

280
00:19:49,110 --> 00:19:55,590
or a engineer's personal blog where you're trying to share details and insights from your day-to-day work.

281
00:19:55,600 --> 00:19:59,029
the things that you're creating with a wider audience.

282
00:19:59,029 --> 00:20:01,669
so that everyone can learn together and grow together as programmers.

283
00:20:01,679 --> 00:20:08,549
and so with that focus i mean so many companies these days are turning to content marketing.

284
00:20:08,549 --> 00:20:14,149
so many developers are publishing individual blogs to talk about their own technical accomplishments.

285
00:20:14,159 --> 00:20:17,990
that i think that now more than ever it's important.

286
00:20:17,990 --> 00:20:21,909
that we have some shared texts as to how to do this well.

287
00:20:21,909 --> 00:20:23,990
so tell us a little bit about your approach to the book.

288
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,270
how did you prepare for it and how did you go about researching for the book?

289
00:20:29,280 --> 00:20:32,470
maybe give us some insights into that process.

290
00:20:32,470 --> 00:20:35,990
the book took six months to write which was a big surprise to me.

291
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:41,110
estimating the length of long projects is hard.

292
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:42,630
and i have a reputation among my friends for saying.

293
00:20:42,630 --> 00:20:44,710
hey we're gonna do this thing it's gonna be super fun.

294
00:20:44,710 --> 00:20:48,230
and we're gonna get it done by the end of winter break or the end of this week or something.

295
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:50,310
and then the project takes a whole year.

296
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:53,669
you sound like every software engineer i've ever met.

297
00:20:53,669 --> 00:20:57,590
well most exactly exactly so the same exact thing happened to you.

298
00:20:57,600 --> 00:21:01,909
it was thanksgiving i was talking to my mother about this idea for this book.

299
00:21:01,909 --> 00:21:05,909
and i said i think it'll be a lot of fun and i'm gonna get it done by the end of winter break.

300
00:21:05,919 --> 00:21:08,789
and then i sat down i wrote an outline.

301
00:21:08,799 --> 00:21:14,789
and i realized that this topic is a so much bigger than i had previously imagined.

302
00:21:14,789 --> 00:21:18,149
and b there were so many fewer resources on it than i thought there would be.

303
00:21:18,159 --> 00:21:20,230
that i set out originally.

304
00:21:20,230 --> 00:21:23,750
i was going to call this thing the technical content development handbook.

305
00:21:23,760 --> 00:21:27,990
which is a substantially narrow approach to the topic.

306
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:32,390
focusing entirely on just making articles for clients.

307
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:35,190
so i arrived at this larger topic.

308
00:21:35,190 --> 00:21:40,230
and i realized that even with the work that i'd been doing over the past at that point past year.

309
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:45,669
i didn't have all of the answers for the questions that i wanted to raise in the book.

310
00:21:45,669 --> 00:21:47,830
and so i thought okay i'm gonna go interview people.

311
00:21:47,830 --> 00:21:49,990
i have a small background in journalism.

312
00:21:49,990 --> 00:21:54,390
i worked at the school newspaper for two semesters one of those is the features editor.

313
00:21:54,390 --> 00:21:59,669
so i was very used to writing stories almost entirely based on interviews.

314
00:21:59,679 --> 00:22:01,750
so i started reaching out to people.

315
00:22:01,750 --> 00:22:03,750
i've always loved sending cold emails.

316
00:22:03,750 --> 00:22:07,430
i know it's kind of weird to love sending cold emails.

317
00:22:07,430 --> 00:22:09,270
but it's just really exciting to reach out to people.

318
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:13,990
who are a thousand times too busy to talk to me and pitch them on something.

319
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,430
and see what they say and you know that's even how i got on the show.

320
00:22:17,430 --> 00:22:19,830
it's a very important part of my writing process.

321
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:23,110
and the process of how i do everything else.

322
00:22:23,110 --> 00:22:28,870
but i sent these emails to almost 100 people 11 of them got back to me.

323
00:22:28,870 --> 00:22:31,990
which was a huge surprise including people whose work i've been reading for years.

324
00:22:31,990 --> 00:22:37,990
who have been incredibly foundational in my understanding of what it means to be a software engineer.

325
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:41,750
and so it was really really cool to get to talk to all of these people.

326
00:22:41,760 --> 00:22:45,270
and then what i did is i transcribed these interviews.

327
00:22:45,280 --> 00:22:50,630
i sent them so i mentioned my mom olio she's a professional copy editor.

328
00:22:50,640 --> 00:22:54,310
and this was a huge advantage in my writing process.

329
00:22:54,310 --> 00:23:00,070
because i was able to get this professional assistance for uh heavily discounted friends and family.

330
00:23:00,070 --> 00:23:05,029
but anyway i went through figuring out how to shape these interviews.

331
00:23:05,029 --> 00:23:10,070
into something that was clean and readable while still authentic to the conversation that we'd been having.

332
00:23:10,070 --> 00:23:13,750
and then at the same time i was working on building up my side of the story.

333
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:17,430
writing down everything i knew about this topic fleshing out my outline.

334
00:23:17,430 --> 00:23:21,110
just like how i was talking about earlier with connecting all these sections together.

335
00:23:21,120 --> 00:23:23,510
so once i had about 30 000 words written.

336
00:23:23,520 --> 00:23:27,350
i exited all of these interviews just like i used to do at the newspaper.

337
00:23:27,360 --> 00:23:31,270
put them in as block quotes contextualized them.

338
00:23:31,270 --> 00:23:33,669
did three rounds of editing one round with beta readers.

339
00:23:33,669 --> 00:23:37,590
one developmental edit with my mom one paper proofread.

340
00:23:37,590 --> 00:23:39,190
then formatted it and put it up.

341
00:23:39,190 --> 00:23:42,789
so that was the writing process it took six months.

342
00:23:42,789 --> 00:23:45,110
during that time you know my college shutdown i had to come home.

343
00:23:45,120 --> 00:23:48,789
which was of course incredibly disruptive to a lot of people.

344
00:23:48,799 --> 00:23:51,909
and i'm one of the more fortunate ones in this situation.

345
00:23:51,919 --> 00:23:55,990
but ultimately that did give me more time to work on the book.

346
00:23:55,990 --> 00:23:57,669
i was basically when i say i was working on it full time.

347
00:23:57,679 --> 00:23:59,430
i don't mean 40 hours a week.

348
00:23:59,430 --> 00:24:01,909
i mean i wake up i work on book i go to sleep.

349
00:24:01,909 --> 00:24:07,590
i wake up i work on book i go to sleep for almost a month.

350
00:24:07,590 --> 00:24:11,510
and that was the time that i just kept revising and revising the content.

351
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:16,789
and that's not something that i think anyone really needs when you're putting together a book.

352
00:24:16,789 --> 00:24:19,830
but it definitely accelerated the process.

353
00:24:19,840 --> 00:24:23,750
and when you're home with maybe not much else to do.

354
00:24:23,750 --> 00:24:27,269
that's a good way to take advantage of an opportunity in a difficult time.

355
00:24:27,279 --> 00:24:31,029
it was you know i definitely wish that the book had taken longer.

356
00:24:31,039 --> 00:24:35,269
because i was busy you know enjoying the world with everyone else.

357
00:24:35,279 --> 00:24:37,430
but unfortunately that wasn't possible.

358
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:41,110
and i'm just glad that i had the you know fortunate background.

359
00:24:41,110 --> 00:24:46,789
to be in a situation where i was able to use this time to focus on creating something.

360
00:24:46,789 --> 00:24:50,470
instead of focusing on surviving which is a much harder struggle that a lot of people are facing.

361
00:24:50,480 --> 00:24:54,550
absolutely. true calling all developers.

362
00:24:54,559 --> 00:24:59,830
there's no telling what you can create when you upgrade your data platform to inner systems.

363
00:24:59,840 --> 00:25:03,190
are you ready to build the applications you want however you want them?

364
00:25:03,200 --> 00:25:06,950
are you ready to develop applications faster than ever?

365
00:25:06,950 --> 00:25:09,269
collaborate build faster and deploy more efficiently.

366
00:25:09,279 --> 00:25:11,990
tomorrow's next breakthroughs are waiting for you today.

367
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:18,149
intersystems iris data platform ready set code start coding for free.

368
00:25:18,159 --> 00:25:23,830
visit intersystems.com try to try iris.

369
00:25:25,590 --> 00:25:30,310
so clearly some aspects of technical writing result in you know maybe a committed markdown file to a repository.

