00:25:41,750 --> 00:25:45,909
and actually producing content that they're going to sell.

374
00:25:45,909 --> 00:25:49,990
share a little bit about your experience of the business side of publishing your own book.

375
00:25:49,990 --> 00:25:51,909
the business side is really interesting.

376
00:25:51,919 --> 00:25:58,310
starting off from the perspective of an individual software engineer wanting to get paid for a piece of writing.

377
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:02,310
i maintain a site called who pays technicalwriters.com.

378
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:06,390
right now it lists 43 places that are known to pay for technical content.

379
00:26:06,390 --> 00:26:10,950
and i'm getting more submissions as i can to figure out how to expand that list.

380
00:26:10,950 --> 00:26:16,070
so there are a lot of places out there that include all the places that i've written for.

381
00:26:16,070 --> 00:26:20,070
that openly solicit hey we want you to write for us.

382
00:26:20,070 --> 00:26:24,870
we want you to work with our editors to develop great pieces of technical content. and we're going to pay for that.

383
00:26:24,880 --> 00:26:28,630
and the rates for those are usually you know at the very low end.

384
00:26:28,630 --> 00:26:30,549
usually about 200 dollars for an article.

385
00:26:30,559 --> 00:26:34,390
and at the high end tends to be about 500.

386
00:26:34,390 --> 00:26:40,149
and you can go even higher from there depending on the value of what you're offering. and how the business is able to use it.

387
00:26:40,159 --> 00:26:46,630
but for me you know even starting out just getting these paychecks wasn't answering my questions.

388
00:26:46,630 --> 00:26:49,669
i always wanted to know where was this money coming from.

389
00:26:49,669 --> 00:26:53,909
you know why are these companies going to invest in this thing that i'm creating.

390
00:26:53,909 --> 00:26:57,350
because if i don't understand that then i'm not going to understand.

391
00:26:57,350 --> 00:26:59,510
how to make something that's going to be really useful for them.

392
00:26:59,520 --> 00:27:03,510
so the business side from the company's perspective can really depend.

393
00:27:03,520 --> 00:27:07,190
and it's all about how they monetize the audience.

394
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:13,510
so some publications like smashing magazine are very sponsor and advertiser focused.

395
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:17,750
they have a little bit of direct sales too in a membership.

396
00:27:17,760 --> 00:27:21,909
so those are three ways that a lot of businesses can make money.

397
00:27:21,919 --> 00:27:29,350
but the majority of technical content in my opinion exists as content marketing.

398
00:27:29,350 --> 00:27:32,710
so if you look at something like twilio they're creating technical content.

399
00:27:32,710 --> 00:27:39,269
that is on one hand useful for you as in you know you're going to learn how to create a cool application.

400
00:27:39,279 --> 00:27:43,190
however that cool application just happens to use twilio's technology.

401
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:45,909
and is maybe going to drive signups.

402
00:27:45,909 --> 00:27:49,669
and you can even think of something like documentation as content marketing.

403
00:27:49,679 --> 00:27:54,230
because if a developer tool has really really great documentation.

404
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:59,510
then that's going to make other developers talk about it have some word of mouth spread.

405
00:27:59,510 --> 00:28:01,510
it's going to make people less likely to turn.

406
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:04,470
if they have problems that they can't figure out.

407
00:28:04,470 --> 00:28:07,590
while they can just go to the documentation figure out that problem.

408
00:28:07,590 --> 00:28:09,750
that reduces your customer support costs.

409
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:15,669
so there's all sorts of benefits to having really great explanations of your stuff.

410
00:28:15,669 --> 00:28:19,269
and that's why you see a lot of companies and develop tools and hosting.

411
00:28:19,269 --> 00:28:27,029
you know twilio digitalocean these sort of companies leading in how they create technical content.

412
00:28:27,029 --> 00:28:30,710
i think another great example is stripe and the integration docs they have.

413
00:28:30,720 --> 00:28:35,990
they don't necessarily do the traditional here's an article that explains stuff.

414
00:28:35,990 --> 00:28:43,350
but focus more on the documentation side and reducing their support costs by creating docs.

415
00:28:43,350 --> 00:28:47,830
so that's you know all of that is one universe where there's plenty of money cycling through technical content.

416
00:28:47,840 --> 00:28:49,909
and then another one is just books.

417
00:28:49,909 --> 00:28:53,750
you know you have major publishers like no starch press o'reilly manning.

418
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:57,830
all of these places putting out technical books.

419
00:28:57,830 --> 00:29:01,510
and if you want to get into that one of the best ways is by having some published articles.

420
00:29:01,510 --> 00:29:06,470
that you can point to as proof that hey i can work with an editor to create great technical content.

421
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:08,149
that people want to read.

422
00:29:08,159 --> 00:29:14,870
so there are a lot of different ways to approach the business side of writing.

423
00:29:14,870 --> 00:29:21,350
and ultimately it just depends on which model you think is going to allow you to do the work you want to do.

424
00:29:21,360 --> 00:29:26,710
and so for me that generally tends to be for the articles working with a publisher.

425
00:29:26,710 --> 00:29:30,230
understanding how the thing i'm creating is going to be valuable for their business.

426
00:29:30,240 --> 00:29:34,630
and then creating something that is first legitimately useful for the reader.

427
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:37,990
and then second useful for their business.

428
00:29:37,990 --> 00:29:40,149
so they're going to be incentivized to purchase and publish it.

429
00:29:40,159 --> 00:29:42,950
and then of course on the book side just self-publishing.

430
00:29:42,960 --> 00:29:45,510
and typically i wouldn't bring up the business side.

431
00:29:45,510 --> 00:29:51,510
because software engineering radio is about the engineering side this isn't software entrepreneurship radio.

432
00:29:51,510 --> 00:29:55,909
however i think in the current world situation it seems like there's some opportunities there.

433
00:29:55,919 --> 00:30:03,590
for people to maybe fill in a pay gap or or be productive if they've been laid off by producing technical content.

434
00:30:03,590 --> 00:30:09,430
have you interacted with people who maybe that's been part of their approach to this situation as well?

435
00:30:09,430 --> 00:30:12,389
yeah, i have and that's why i did.

436
00:30:12,389 --> 00:30:17,190
you know of course make my book free to anyone who has been laid off.

437
00:30:17,190 --> 00:30:20,950
or as a student for any other reason can't purchase it.

438
00:30:20,950 --> 00:30:24,230
because you know this writing technical content has been a real bridge for me.

439
00:30:24,240 --> 00:30:27,750
into proper full-time software engineering work.

440
00:30:27,760 --> 00:30:31,029
and so if this book can serve as a bridge for other people.

441
00:30:31,039 --> 00:30:33,350
then that's you know that's fantastic.

